The overview below comes from the four documents linked after it,
which I read today. Where I give my own view, I say so.

The model
---------

1. Topic branches. Each series Junio picks up goes on its own branch,
   named with the author's initials and a topic, e.g. ai/topic. It
   forks from the oldest branch it will land on. A reroll replaces
   the branch. He compares the versions with git range-diff.

2. Integration branches, from most to least settled:

   - maint: fixes for the last release. Never rewound.
   - master: the next release. Never rewound, and meant to be "more
     stable than any tagged releases".
   - next: topics that look ready. It is rewound only once per release
     cycle, early in the cycle.
   - seen: everything else he found worth testing. It is rebuilt from
     the topic branches every time he integrates. In SubmittingPatches'
     words, "Being in 'seen' has no other meaning."

   A topic graduates by merging its branch upward. Nobody may base
   work on next or seen; gitworkflows calls seen a throw-away
   integration branch.

3. Timeboxes. A topic waits in next for at least 7 calendar days before
   it goes to master. maintain-git says a topic in seen that has been
   inactive for 3 weeks may be discarded. SubmittingPatches says the
   same of one with unaddressed review after 4 weeks.

4. Replace, then append. Until a topic reaches next, each version
   replaces the last whole. After it reaches next, fixes come as
   incremental patches on top.

5. "What's cooking in git.git". This report goes to the list a few
   times a week. Its script, Meta/cook, walks the topic merges in
   master..seen and keeps one entry per topic, with Junio's verdict
   ("Will merge to 'next'.", "Expecting a reroll.") carried forward
   from the previous issue. I explained how its entries link commits
   to mail in my reply on "Supporting status trailers in emails" [1].
   It covers only topics he applied. Mail he never picks up is
   tracked nowhere, which is how Git keeps the noise down.

6. Contributor duties. A contributor bases work on maint or master,
   never on next or seen. They try a merge into seen and next and say
   in the cover letter which topics conflict. They answer review on
   the list before sending a reroll, and send at most one version a
   day. Credit goes in trailers: Reviewed-by may be given only by the
   reviewer, and Helped-by, Suggested-by and Reported-by name the
   others.

7. The maintainer's tools are themselves published. The todo branch of
   git.git holds Meta/cook, Meta/Reintegrate (which rebuilds next and
   seen from the topics), whats-cooking.txt with its full history,
   and MaintNotes. MaintNotes is "A note from the maintainer", which
   Junio posts to the list now and then to explain all of the above.

Links
-----

- SubmittingPatches, the contributor's side:
  https://git-scm.com/docs/SubmittingPatches
- howto/maintain-git, Junio's daily routine and branch rules:
  https://github.com/git/git/blob/master/Documentation/howto/maintain-git.adoc
- gitworkflows, the branch model in general terms:
  https://git-scm.com/docs/gitworkflows
- MyFirstContribution, a tutorial from patch to reroll:
  https://git-scm.com/docs/MyFirstContribution
- The todo branch, with MaintNotes, cook and whats-cooking.txt:
  https://github.com/git/git/tree/todo
- The list archive. Search it for "What's cooking" to read real issues:
  https://lore.kernel.org/git/

How it maps here
----------------

- trunk is master. There are no releases, so nothing plays maint.
- b/dev sits between next and seen. You rebuild it freely, as Junio
  rebuilds seen. CLAUDE.md, however, calls it "more mature than a
  random list patch" and lets work build on it, as next allows.
- The list is the topic store. Change-Id headers from format-patch.sh
  join a commit to its mail ("notmuch search changeid:<id>"), which is
  the job git-am notes do for Meta/cook.
- The rules already line up in several places: one logical change per
  commit, the area prefix, new threads for each vN, an interdiff on
  each reroll ("New patch series must have an interdiff" [2]), and
  "-A trunk -B b/dev" so each topic stays separable.

Ideas, in the order I recommend
-------------------------------

These are my proposals, not anything Git prescribes.

1. Generate a "What's cooking" report. A script would walk
   trunk..b/dev, group commits into topics by series, and use each
   Change-Id to find the topic's thread in notmuch. It would then add
   your latest verdict per topic and the open series not yet on b/dev,
   and mail the result to the list. This is the WIP view I proposed in
   "Re: [RFC] planning and organizing tasks" [3]. It would replace the
   folder-based ranking in the patch-triage skill on b/dev. It depends
   on how verdicts are written. You left that open in "Supporting
   status trailers in emails" [4], and settling it is the one decision
   this needs from you.

2. Build b/dev from topic bookmarks. Keep one bookmark per series
   (t/<topic>) on upstream, and make b/dev a jj merge of them. Then
   dropping or replacing a topic means rebuilding the merge, which is
   what Reintegrate does for seen. jj makes this cheap, since a
   rewritten topic rebases its merge automatically. I would not split
   b/dev into next and seen yet. At today's volume I think one staging
   tier is enough; a second tier pays off once things are often
   pulled back out of b/dev.

3. Adopt Git's inactivity rule. A series with unanswered review and no
   activity for 3 weeks is tagged stale and drops out of the default
   searches. Derive the tag from dates, the way the superseded tag is
   derived in "[PATCH 0/3] notmuch: Tag superseded series and hide
   them" [5]. This goes straight at the noise from old patches you
   raised in [4], and it needs no tagging by hand.

4. Write a note from the maintainer. That is one short file: the
   branches and what being on each one means, the timeboxes, and what
   you expect from a contributor. doc/mail-workflow.md is stale, and
   this would replace it. lab-process-mail and patch-triage could cite
   it instead of restating the rules.

5. Leave out the parts that do not fit: maint and releases, the 7-day
   wait in next (with no outside users, b/dev gets its testing from
   use), and the merge-fix refs that patch up semantic conflicts
   between topics. Revisit them if they start to hurt.

If you want any of these, say which and I will post a series. 1 and 3
are the most useful. 3 can start now. 1 waits on the verdict format.

